--TEST--
an intermediate level of a nested element write is refused where it is written when it is not an array of its own, for a scalar, a `mixed` and an untested `?array<T>` alike — `rule:types/arrays`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/arrays` separates every level of a nested element write, innermost
// first, and each level's separated copy is written back into the level above
// it — so an *intermediate* level has to be an array of its own for there to
// be anything to separate. The level that is not one is refused where it is
// written, exactly as a first-level subscript with no element type already is
// (`a-subscript-needs-an-array-to-read-from`), and this is the same rule asked
// one level in: a scalar element, a `mixed` one, and an untested `?array<T>`
// one, whose `null` is a level the write-back has nowhere to land in. `unset()`
// separates the chain the same way a write does, so it answers the same.
array<int> $flat = ["0" => 1];
array<mixed> $loose = ["0" => 1];
array<?array<int>> $sparse = ["0" => null];
array<array<int>> $grid = ["0" => ["0" => 1]];

$flat["0"]["1"] = 5;
$loose["0"]["1"] = 5;
$sparse["0"]["1"] = 5;
$grid["0"]["1"]["2"] = 5;

unset($flat["0"]["1"]);
unset($sparse["0"]["1"]);

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0482]: `int` cannot be subscripted
   --> case.nvs:16:1
%A
error[E0482]: `mixed` cannot be subscripted
   --> case.nvs:17:1
%A
error[E0482]: `null|array<int>` cannot be subscripted
   --> case.nvs:18:1
%A
error[E0482]: `int` cannot be subscripted
   --> case.nvs:19:1
%A
error[E0482]: `int` cannot be subscripted
   --> case.nvs:21:7
%A
error[E0482]: `null|array<int>` cannot be subscripted
   --> case.nvs:22:7
%A
error: aborting due to 6 errors
